  "tldr": "The paper determines the fixed-sample minimax scale at which observational data can detect any nonconstant binary-outcome CATE when the propensity, baseline regression, and CATE have separate rough Hölder indices. A known-propensity projection test gives the oracle rate, while an explicit profiled fourth-order statistic gives a uniformly calibrated all-regime upper envelope and already matches the oracle in a broad asymmetric region. The central open content is to replace that envelope by the sharp multiresolution rate, prove a same-class converse whose every null draw has exactly constant CATE, and derive the necessary-and-sufficient frontier at which revealing the true propensity changes the radius. The deliverable is a total finite-data test and a detectability benchmark, not a certificate for a fitted causal forest and not evidence of homogeneity after non-rejection.",
