  "related_work": "Kennedy, Balakrishnan, Robins and Wasserman (2024), Theorems 1--2 and Sections 3--4, derive a localized fuzzy-hypothesis lower bound and higher-order local-polynomial estimator for pointwise CATE estimation; their loss, localization, and design support do not yield a global constant-CATE test on the present exact uniform design. Neykov, Balakrishnan and Wasserman (2021), Theorems 4.1 and 5.1, derive matching conditional-independence radii using binned degenerate U-statistics, but their total-variation separation and joint-law smoothness do not retain separate \\(e,m_0,\\tau\\) indices. Lundborg, Kim, Shah and Samworth (2024), Theorems 6--7, obtain projected-covariance validity and minimax power under common smoothness and nondegeneracy conditions; the present null adds scalar profiling and propensity--baseline binning bias. Dukes, Stensrud, Brioschi and Hudson (2026), Theorems 4 and 6, directly test quantitative homogeneity with fixed and local power over structured rules, but do not calculate a uniform Hölder critical radius. Yu (2026), Theorem 1, removes null degeneracy by intra-fold splitting under supplied learner rates, whereas the present class supplies no nuisance procedure. McGrath and Mukherjee (2026), Theorem 1 and Corollary 1, derive target-specific nuisance resolutions for a signed scalar expected covariance; cancellation of that scalar under heterogeneous alternatives prevents direct transfer to centered squared CATE energy. Kotekal and Kundu (2025) show that a proxy can improve heteroskedasticity testing with a rough mean, but the causal Bernoulli likelihood adds unknown treatment selection and omits their Gaussian-noise leverage. Hahn (1998) provides the regular ATE ancillarity benchmark; the proposed known-versus-unknown propensity theorem concerns a null-degenerate nonregular test.",
  "technical_internal_limitation": "The simple sign mixture \\(e_{\\sigma}=1/2+\\sigma v b\\), \\(m_{0,\\sigma}=3/8+\\sigma u b\\), and \\(\\tau_{\\sigma}\\equiv1/8\\) matches one-observation cell probabilities after averaging to a heterogeneous law, but a shared sign creates detectable two-observation covariance. It is a diagnostic identity, not a lower bound. The single-resolution sparse-bin rate is likewise only an upper envelope and may be an artifact of third- and fourth-order Hoeffding projections.",
